D={
"Ibn Sina (Avicenna)":("Ibn Sina (980–1037), born near Bukhara, memorised the Quran by 10 and was treating patients by 18. He served Samanid and Buyid rulers as physician and vizier. His Canon of Medicine, a five-volume encyclopaedia, described contagion, clinical trials of drugs and hundreds of medicines, and was a standard textbook in Europe until the 17th century. His Book of Healing covered philosophy, logic and science, integrating Aristotle with Islamic thought.","Islam; Abbasid Caliphate; Aristotle; Ibn Sina's Canon of Medicine; Islamic Golden Age"),
"Al-Biruni":("Abu Rayhan al-Biruni (973–1048), a polymath from Khwarazm, accompanied Mahmud of Ghazni to India, learned Sanskrit and wrote Kitab al-Hind (about 1030), a remarkably objective study of Indian religion, philosophy, astronomy, mathematics and customs. He calculated the Earth's radius with impressive accuracy using a mountain and the horizon, and wrote on mineralogy and pharmacology.","Mahmud of Ghazni raids Somnath; Islam; Hinduism; Islamic Golden Age"),
"Bhaskara II":("Bhaskaracharya (1114–1185), head of the Ujjain observatory, wrote the Siddhanta Shiromani, which includes Lilavati (arithmetic, reportedly named for his daughter), Bijaganita (algebra) and astronomy. He solved Pell's equation with the cyclic chakravala method, described ideas resembling the derivative and Rolle's theorem, and calculated the time Earth takes to orbit the Sun.","Aryabhata; Brahmagupta; Hinduism"),
"Ibn Battuta":("Ibn Battuta (1304–1369), a Moroccan legal scholar, left Tangier in 1325 for Mecca and kept travelling for 29 years, covering about 120,000 km — Africa, Arabia, Anatolia, Central Asia, India, the Maldives, Southeast Asia and China. In Delhi he served as a judge under Muhammad bin Tughlaq for eight years. His Rihla describes the Mali Empire, the Delhi Sultanate and the Black Death.","Delhi Sultanate; Tughlaq dynasty; Mali Empire; Black Death; Islam; Marco Polo in China"),
"Kabir":("Kabir (about 1440–1518), a weaver of Varanasi raised in a Muslim family, became a key Bhakti poet-saint. His dohas (couplets), in simple Hindi, mocked caste, idol worship, ritual and religious hypocrisy of both Hindus and Muslims, teaching devotion to a formless God within. His verses are included in the Guru Granth Sahib, and the Kabir Panth follows him.","Bhakti movement; Sufism; Guru Nanak; Sikhism; Delhi Sultanate; Lodi dynasty"),
"Guru Nanak":("Guru Nanak (1469–1539), born at Talwandi (Nankana Sahib, Pakistan), had a spiritual experience at 30 and declared 'There is no Hindu, there is no Muslim'. He travelled widely (udasis) across India, Sri Lanka, Tibet and Arabia, teaching devotion to one God (Ik Onkar), honest work, sharing (vand chhakna) and equality, and founded langar. He settled at Kartarpur and was the first of the ten Sikh Gurus.","Sikhism; Bhakti movement; Kabir; Lodi dynasty; Babur; Guru Granth Sahib (Kartarpur Bir)"),
"Mirabai":("Mirabai (about 1498–1547), a Rajput princess of Merta married into the royal family of Mewar, devoted herself to Krishna, whom she regarded as her true husband. Defying her in-laws, she sang and danced in temples among commoners; legends say they tried to poison her. Hundreds of her bhajans in Rajasthani and Braj are still sung.","Bhakti movement; Rajput kingdoms of Mewar (Sisodia); Hinduism; Kabir"),
"Leonardo da Vinci":("Leonardo da Vinci (1452–1519), born near Florence, trained in Verrocchio's workshop and worked for the Medici, Milan's Sforza dukes and the French king Francis I. He painted The Last Supper and the Mona Lisa, and filled about 7,000 surviving notebook pages, in mirror writing, with studies of anatomy (dissecting about 30 corpses), flying machines, water, geology and engineering. He embodies the 'Renaissance man'.","Renaissance; Leonardo's Codex Leicester; Christianity"),
"Nicolaus Copernicus":("Nicolaus Copernicus (1473–1543), a Polish canon and astronomer, proposed in De revolutionibus (1543) that the Earth and planets revolve around the Sun, rather than everything around the Earth (the Ptolemaic model). The book was published as he lay dying. His heliocentric model began the Scientific Revolution.","Scientific Revolution and Enlightenment; Galileo Galilei; Isaac Newton; Aryabhata; Christianity"),
"Galileo Galilei":("Galileo Galilei (1564–1642), an Italian mathematician, improved the telescope and in 1610 discovered Jupiter's moons, Venus's phases and mountains on the Moon, supporting Copernicus. He studied falling bodies and pendulums, founding modern physics through experiment. The Roman Inquisition tried him in 1633 and forced him to recant; he spent his last years under house arrest. The Church acknowledged its error in 1992.","Nicolaus Copernicus; Isaac Newton; Scientific Revolution and Enlightenment; Christianity; Protestant Reformation"),
"Isaac Newton":("Isaac Newton (1643–1727) of England developed calculus (independently of Leibniz), discovered that white light is a spectrum, and built the reflecting telescope. In Principia Mathematica (1687) he described three laws of motion and universal gravitation, explaining both falling apples and planetary orbits. He later ran the Royal Mint and was President of the Royal Society. He also spent years on alchemy and biblical prophecy.","Scientific Revolution and Enlightenment; Galileo Galilei; Nicolaus Copernicus; Albert Einstein; Christianity"),
"Charles Darwin":("Charles Darwin (1809–1882) sailed on HMS Beagle (1831–36), studying fossils and wildlife, including Galápagos finches and tortoises. After 20 years of research, prompted by Alfred Russel Wallace's similar ideas, he published On the Origin of Species (1859), arguing species evolve by natural selection. The Descent of Man (1871) applied this to humans, sparking debates with religious thinkers.","Industrial Revolution; Human–chimpanzee split; Homo sapiens (early); Christianity"),
"Raja Ram Mohan Roy":("Raja Ram Mohan Roy (1772–1833), a Bengali scholar fluent in Sanskrit, Persian, Arabic and English, campaigned against sati, polygamy and child marriage and for women's property rights and modern education. He founded the Brahmo Samaj (1828), a monotheistic reform movement, and newspapers. His campaign led to the abolition of sati (1829). He died in Bristol, England, while representing the Mughal emperor.","Abolition of sati; Brahmo Samaj and Arya Samaj; East India Company rule; Hinduism"),
"Savitribai Phule":("Savitribai Phule (1831–1897), married at nine to Jyotirao Phule, who taught her to read, became India's first woman teacher. In 1848 they opened a school for girls in Pune, facing people throwing dung at her on the way. They opened schools for Dalit children, a home for widows and their children, and founded the Satyashodhak Samaj. She died nursing plague patients in 1897.","Third plague pandemic; British Raj; B. R. Ambedkar"),
"Swami Vivekananda":("Narendranath Datta (1863–1902), a disciple of the mystic Ramakrishna, became Swami Vivekananda. His speech at the Parliament of the World's Religions in Chicago in 1893, opening 'Sisters and brothers of America', introduced Vedanta and yoga to the West. He founded the Ramakrishna Mission (1897) for social service and inspired Indian nationalism. National Youth Day is on his birthday.","Hinduism; British Raj; Rabindranath Tagore"),
"Rabindranath Tagore":("Rabindranath Tagore (1861–1941), Bengali poet, novelist, composer and painter, became the first non-European Nobel laureate in Literature (1913) for Gitanjali. He wrote the national anthems of India (Jana Gana Mana) and Bangladesh (Amar Shonar Bangla), founded Visva-Bharati University at Santiniketan, and returned his knighthood after the Jallianwala Bagh massacre. He called Gandhi 'Mahatma'.","Jallianwala Bagh massacre; Partition of Bengal; Mahatma Gandhi; British Raj; Brahmo Samaj and Arya Samaj"),
"Marie Curie":("Marie Skłodowska Curie (1867–1934), born in Warsaw, studied in Paris, where women could attend university. With Pierre Curie she discovered polonium (named after Poland) and radium, and coined 'radioactivity'. She won the Nobel Prize in Physics (1903) and Chemistry (1911), the first person to win two. In World War I she developed mobile X-ray units. She died of radiation-induced illness.","Albert Einstein; World War I"),
"Albert Einstein":("Albert Einstein (1879–1955), working in a Swiss patent office, published four groundbreaking papers in 1905, including special relativity and E=mc². His general relativity (1915) described gravity as the curvature of spacetime. He won the 1921 Nobel Prize for the photoelectric effect. A Jew, he fled Nazi Germany in 1933 to the US, and his 1939 letter to Roosevelt led to the Manhattan Project, which he later regretted. He corresponded with Tagore and admired Gandhi.","Franklin D. Roosevelt; Adolf Hitler; Atomic bombings of Hiroshima and Nagasaki; Rabindranath Tagore; Isaac Newton; Judaism"),
"C. V. Raman":("Chandrasekhara Venkata Raman (1888–1970) discovered in 1928 in Calcutta that light changes wavelength when scattered by molecules — the Raman effect — winning the 1930 Nobel Prize in Physics, the first Asian science Nobel. He founded the Raman Research Institute in Bangalore. India celebrates National Science Day on 28 February, the day of his discovery.","British Raj; Srinivasa Ramanujan"),
"Srinivasa Ramanujan":("Srinivasa Ramanujan (1887–1920), a largely self-taught mathematician from Kumbakonam, sent a letter of theorems to G. H. Hardy at Cambridge in 1913. Hardy brought him to England, where he produced remarkable results on partitions, infinite series, mock theta functions and the number 1729. He became a Fellow of the Royal Society but fell ill and died in India at 32. His notebooks still inspire research.","British Raj; C. V. Raman; World War I"),
"A. P. J. Abdul Kalam":("Avul Pakir Jainulabdeen Abdul Kalam (1931–2015), from Rameswaram, sold newspapers as a boy and became an aerospace scientist. He led the development of India's first satellite launch vehicle (SLV-3, 1980) and the Agni and Prithvi missiles, earning the title 'Missile Man', and played a key role in the 1998 Pokhran nuclear tests. As President (2002–07) he was called the 'People's President' and inspired students.","Indian Constitution adopted; Chandrayaan-3 lands on the Moon; Islam"),
"Enheduanna":("Enheduanna (about 2285–2250 BCE), daughter of Sargon of Akkad, was appointed high priestess of the moon god Nanna at Ur, uniting Akkadian and Sumerian religion. She composed hymns, including 'The Exaltation of Inanna' and 42 temple hymns, and is the earliest known author identified by name. She was briefly exiled during a revolt but restored.","Akkadian Empire; Sumer; Sargon of Akkad; Mesopotamian religion; Cuneiform"),
"Imhotep":("Imhotep (about 2650–2600 BCE) served Pharaoh Djoser as chancellor, high priest of Ra and architect. He designed the Step Pyramid at Saqqara, the first monumental stone building, stacking six mastabas. Later Egyptians credited him with medicine and wisdom, and he was deified; the Greeks identified him with Asclepius.","Ancient Egypt; Old Kingdom of Egypt; Ancient Egyptian religion; Great Pyramid built"),
"Akhenaten":("Amenhotep IV (r. about 1353–1336 BCE) renamed himself Akhenaten, promoting the sun disc Aten as the supreme or only god. He closed temples of Amun, built a new capital at Amarna, and encouraged naturalistic art showing his family. His wife was Nefertiti. After his death, his son Tutankhamun restored the old gods and Akhenaten was condemned as a heretic.","Ancient Egypt; New Kingdom of Egypt; Akhenaten's religious revolution; Ancient Egyptian religion; Amarna letters"),
"Zoroaster":("Zoroaster (Zarathustra), a priest-prophet of ancient Iran, probably lived between 1500 and 1000 BCE. He taught that Ahura Mazda, the wise lord, is the supreme god, opposed by the destructive spirit Angra Mainyu, and that humans must choose good thoughts, words and deeds. His hymns, the Gathas, are in the Avesta. His ideas of heaven, hell, judgement and a saviour may have influenced Judaism, Christianity and Islam. Parsis in India follow his faith.","Zoroastrianism; Achaemenid Persian Empire; Sasanian Empire; Avestan and Old Persian"),
"Bimbisara":("Bimbisara (r. about 558–491 BCE) of the Haryanka dynasty made Magadha the strongest state in north India. He annexed Anga, married princesses of Kosala, Vaishali and Madra, and built Rajagriha as capital. He was a patron of the Buddha and is also honoured by Jains. His son Ajatashatru imprisoned him and he died in captivity.","Magadha; Haryanka dynasty; Gautama Buddha; Mahavira; Buddhism"),
"Pericles":("Pericles (about 495–429 BCE), an aristocrat and orator, led Athens during its golden age. He strengthened democracy by paying jurors, used the Delian League's treasury to build the Parthenon and Acropolis, and patronised artists like Phidias. He led Athens into the Peloponnesian War and died in the Plague of Athens.","Classical Greece; Athenian democracy; Peloponnesian War; Plague of Athens; Socrates"),
"Herodotus":("Herodotus (about 484–425 BCE), from Halicarnassus, wrote the Histories, investigating the causes of the Persian Wars. He travelled to Egypt, Babylon and the Black Sea, collecting stories about customs. Cicero called him the 'Father of History'.","Persian Wars; Classical Greece; Achaemenid Persian Empire; Scythians"),
"Hippocrates":("Hippocrates of Kos (about 460–370 BCE) argued that diseases have natural causes. The Hippocratic Corpus describes observation and prognosis, and the Hippocratic Oath still inspires medical ethics.","Classical Greece; Plague of Athens"),
"Sun Tzu":("Sun Tzu, traditionally a general of the state of Wu in the 6th century BCE, is credited with The Art of War, a 13-chapter treatise. It teaches that the best victory is won without fighting, through deception, intelligence and knowing yourself and the enemy. It influenced Mao, Japanese strategists and modern business.","Zhou dynasty; Confucius"),
"Hannibal":("Hannibal Barca (247–183 BCE) of Carthage marched an army with elephants across the Pyrenees and Alps in 218 BCE. He destroyed Roman armies at the Trebia, Lake Trasimene and Cannae (216 BCE), where about 50,000 Romans died. He was recalled to defend Carthage and lost to Scipio at Zama (202 BCE). He later took poison to avoid capture.","Carthage; Punic Wars; Roman Republic; Phoenicia"),
"Euclid":("Euclid of Alexandria (about 300 BCE) wrote the Elements, 13 books building geometry and number theory from definitions and axioms. It was used as a textbook for 2,000 years and remains a model of logical proof.","Ptolemaic dynasty; Classical Greece; Archimedes"),
"Sima Qian":("Sima Qian (about 145–86 BCE), grand historian of the Han, chose castration over execution after defending a disgraced general, to finish his Records of the Grand Historian. It covers 2,000 years of Chinese history with biographies.","Han dynasty; Qin Shi Huang; Confucianism"),
"Jesus of Nazareth":("Jesus of Nazareth (about 4 BCE – 30/33 CE), a Jewish teacher from Galilee, preached the Kingdom of God, love for enemies and care for the poor, and was known as a healer. He was crucified in Jerusalem under Pontius Pilate. His followers believed he rose from the dead and was the Messiah, founding Christianity, now the world's largest religion.","Christianity; Judaism; Roman Empire; Crucifixion of Jesus; Augustus"),
"Prophet Muhammad":("Muhammad (about 570–632), born in Mecca, an orphan raised by his uncle, became a trader. From about 610 he received revelations that became the Quran. Persecuted, he moved to Medina in 622 (the Hijra), led the community through battles with Mecca, and took Mecca in 630. By his death Arabia was united under Islam.","Islam; Hijra and rise of Islam; Battle of Badr; Rashidun Caliphate; Shia and Sunni split"),
"Empress Wu Zetian":("Wu Zetian (624–705) was a concubine of Emperor Taizong who became Emperor Gaozong's empress, ruling behind him and then for her sons. In 690 she declared her own Zhou dynasty, China's only female emperor. She expanded the examination system, promoted Buddhism and used secret police. She was deposed in 705.","Tang dynasty; Buddhism"),
"Xuanzang":("Xuanzang (602–664), a Chinese monk, travelled to India (629–645), studied at Nalanda and returned with 657 texts. His travelogue describes Harsha's India.","Tang dynasty; Xuanzang's journey to India; Nalanda founded; Harsha; Buddhism"),
}
